{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0290-machine-learning-for-sports-betting-should.md", "equation": "Eq. 1 (Kelly): k = (pb − q)/b, where p = model probability, q = 1 − p, b = odds − 1. Eq. 2 (perfect calibration): P(Ŷ = Y | P̂ = p) = p ∀ p ∈ [0,1]. Eq. 3 (classwise-ECE): (1/k)Σ_i Σ_j ( | , with k classes, m bins of equal width over [0,1], bin-size-weighted. Eq. 4 (LR): P(Y_i = C | X_i) = exp(X_iβ)/(1 + exp(X_iβ)). Eq. 5: negative log-likelihood −Σ[y_i log f(X_iβ) + (1−y_i) log(1−f(X_iβ))]. Eq. 6 (SVM): argmin_w {(1/n)Σ max(0, 1 − y_i f(x_i)) + C w^T w}. Kelly simulation (Table 1): K = (P × Bankroll − (1−P))/Bankroll (i.e., Kelly fraction on stake scale), stake = (1/8)×K×Bankroll (eighth-Kelly) or $100 fixed. Assumptions: bookmaker implied probability treated as proxy for true probability (citing Wheatcroft 2020) in the scatter analysis; covariate shift tested at 1% significance; ≥80% of ECE bins must be non-empty else ECE := 1 (prevents \"predict the base rate\" degenerate calibration).", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9124}
