{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0298-bayesian-conwaymaxwellpoisson-model-with-spikeand-slab.md", "equation": "Poisson models of football scores assume equidispersion (mean = variance), yet team-level goal frequencies across a season show substantial heterogeneity in dispersion. The authors generalize the Maher (1982) Poisson goal model with a Conway-Maxwell-Poisson (CMP) likelihood whose team-specific dispersion parameters ν_i carry a spike-and-slab (SAS) prior: spike at ν=1 (equidispersed Poisson regime), slab LogNormal around 1 (non-equidispersed regime). This yields interpretable posterior probabilities P(Z_i=1|Y) of each team's non-equidispersion plus the magnitude of departure, with a thresholding rule for classification. Inference via a tailored Metropolis-within-Gibbs sampler handling the doubly-intractable CMP likelihood via the Exchange algorithm. Evaluated on simulations and 5 EPL seasons (2020/21–2024/25).", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9124}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0298-bayesian-conwaymaxwellpoisson-model-with-spikeand-slab.md", "equation": "μ,ν) = (μ^y/y!)^ν / Z_f(μ,ν); Z_f(μ,ν) = Σ_{x=0}^∞ (μ^x/x!)^ν. Eq. 2: ν=1 recovers Poisson (Z_f = e^μ). Moments: E(Y) ≈ μ + 1/(2ν) − 1/2, Var(Y) ≈ μ/ν. Eq. 3: team-specific ν on scoring team. Eq. 4: log(μ^H_{i,j}) = α_i + β_j + γ; log(μ^A_{j,i}) = α_j + β_i. Eq. 5: π(Z_i | p_i) ~ Bern(p_i); π(p_i) ~ Beta(a,b); π(η_i) ~ N(0,σ_η²); ν_i := exp(Z_i η_i). Eq. 6: Σα_i = Σβ_i = 0. Eqs. 9–10: correlated (α_i, η_i) proposal, ρ=0.85. Eq. 11: A_Z exchange acceptance for Z_i. Eq. 17: WAIC = −2(lppd − p_WAIC). Eq. 18: IGN = −log2(f̃(y*)). Assumptions: conditionally independent scores (dependence only via team parameters; justified by Egidi et al. 2018 / McHale & Scarf 2011 low correlation); dispersion is a team-season trait; home advantage γ constant across teams; seasons analyzed independently (team turnover); equidispersed teams sit at posterior P(Z_i=1)≈0.25 baseline, not 0.", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9124}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0298-bayesian-conwaymaxwellpoisson-model-with-spikeand-slab.md", "equation": "(a) Dispersion recovery: boxplots of P(Z_i=1|Y) vs ν_true (Fig. 3); TP/FP classification at E[P(Z_i=1)]>0.5 (Table 1). (b) Model fit: WAIC Poisson vs CMP-SAS vs CMP-Full at ν_true ∈ {0.3, 0.6, 0.9} / {4, 2, 1.2}, 3 replicates (Table 2). (c) EPL 2023/24: posterior ν_i and P(Z_i=1) per team (Fig. 5, Table 3), attack/defence shifts vs Poisson (Fig. 6), WAIC in-sample (Table 4), out-of-sample IGN on 190 games (Table 5), Man Utd–Fulham 1–2 case study (Fig. 7). (d) Five seasons: dispersed-team summaries (Fig. 8), WAIC (Table 6), out-of-sample IGN × 3 markets (Table 7).", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9124}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0298-bayesian-conwaymaxwellpoisson-model-with-spikeand-slab.md", "equation": "Simulation (Table 1): FP ≈ 0.04–0.16 (avg ~10%) stable across k; TP = 1.00 at ν_true=0.2, 0.94 at 0.3, drops sharply (0.58 at 0.5, 0.14 at 0.8); under-dispersed TP 0.94–0.98 at ν_true ≥ 3.2, 0.42 at 1.6. WAIC (Table 2): extreme dispersion — CMP models vastly beat Poisson (e.g., ν=0.3: Poisson 2836.5 vs CMP-SAS 2640.5, rep 1); mild (ν=0.6/2): CMP-SAS best all replicates; near-equidispersion: Poisson best but CMP-SAS very close (2130.5 vs 2131.3 at ν=0.9), CMP-Full decays more — SAS robustness confirmed. EPL 2023/24 (threshold 0.5): 3 over-dispersed (Fulham P(Z)=0.94, ν̂=0.323; Brighton 0.58, ν̂=0.729; Wolves 0.51, ν̂=0.766) + 1 under-dispersed (Nott'm Forest 0.53, ν̂=1.395); threshold 0.4 adds Liverpool, Bournemouth, Chelsea. Fulham's attack parameter shifts from −0.090 (Poisson) to −1.831 (CMP-SAS) — extreme overdispersion (boom/bust scoring) masked by Poisson. In-sample WAIC 2023/24 (Ta ...", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9124}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0298-bayesian-conwaymaxwellpoisson-model-with-spikeand-slab.md", "equation": "(a) Soccer goals, not NFL points — counts are low (mean ~1.4/team/match); NFL team scores (mean ~22) are a different count regime where CMP behavior must be re-validated. (b) Conditional independence assumption under-predicts draws (authors' own note) — the NFL analog (correlated team scores within a game) is stronger in football with fewer possessions; a Dixon-Coles-style dependence correction is flagged as future work. (c) No recency weighting — all matches weighted equally (authors flag this). (d) Detection power is limited near equidispersion: TP collapses for 0.7≤ν≤1.2 — mild dispersion is hard to identify with 38 games/team; NFL's 17-game season is even thinner. (e) Posterior baseline P(Z_i=1)≈0.25 for equidispersed teams means the 0.5 threshold is doing real work; threshold choice (0.4 vs 0.5) changes conclusions. (f) MCMC cost: 250k iterations × exchange auxiliary sampling — heav ...", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9124}
